From: EasyStick maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sdio: poll the raw packet-length register after OPEN_DATA_PATH

The single 100 ms drain added by 0005 only proves the C6 boot-up frame is
absent at exactly one instant.  It cannot distinguish "C6 never queues the
frame" from "C6 queues it after 100 ms" from "the SDIO card interrupt never
reaches esp_handle_isr() so the driver never notices a frame that IS queued".
Replace the single drain with a bounded poll of the same CMD52 length
register used by esp_get_len_from_slave(), logging every sample.  This is
temporary board-port telemetry: it records only a byte count and elapsed
time, never Wi-Fi credentials or payload contents.  Normal traffic
continues to rely on the SDIO card interrupt; this loop only runs once,
during probe, and only while diagnosing the missing boot frame.

---
 sdio/esp_sdio.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sdio/esp_sdio.c b/sdio/esp_sdio.c
--- a/sdio/esp_sdio.c
+++ b/sdio/esp_sdio.c
@@ -862,9 +862,21 @@ static int esp_probe(struct sdio_func *func,
 	ret = generate_slave_intr(context, BIT(ESP_OPEN_DATA_PATH));
 	esp_info("OPEN_DATA_PATH write completed: ret=%d\n", ret);
 	if (!ret) {
+		unsigned int poll_iter;
+		u32 polled_len = 0;
+
 		/* The first C6 BOOTUP frame predates the initial producer snapshot. */
 		context->rx_byte_count = 0;
-		msleep(100);
+		for (poll_iter = 0; poll_iter < 10; poll_iter++) {
+			int poll_ret = esp_get_len_from_slave(context, &polled_len,
+							       ACQUIRE_LOCK);
+
+			esp_info("BOOT_POLL: iter=%u elapsed_ms=%u len=%u ret=%d\n",
+				 poll_iter, poll_iter * 300, polled_len, poll_ret);
+			if (!poll_ret && polled_len)
+				break;
+			msleep(300);
+		}
 		esp_process_new_packet_intr(context->adapter);
 	}
 
